  <!-- ============ CONTROLS ============ -->
  <section id="controls" aria-labelledby="controls-h">
    <h2 id="controls-h">Controls</h2>
    <p>Listed in signal order. Numbers follow the Versio panel: by column, top to bottom. Every knob has a CV input with the same number, which adds to the knob's position.</p>
    <div class="ref">
      <article><span class="num">1</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Predelay</h3><span class="range">0–666 ms · free-running</span></div>
        <p>How long after the kick the rumble starts. Pushing the swell later moves it off the kick and into the gap before the next one.</p>
        <ul><li>Squared taper: the first 60% of the knob covers 0–250 ms, where most rumbles live.</li><li>666 ms is a quarter note at 90 BPM. Not synced to tempo: dial it by ear, or try a dotted eighth (about 346 ms at 130 BPM) for repeats that drift 3 against 4.</li><li>Smoothed, so turning it doesn't zipper.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">2</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Feedback</h3><span class="range">0–100%</span></div>
        <p>Feeds the predelay back into itself. At 0 you get one swell per kick; turned up, each repeat re-triggers the smear at the predelay interval, for a denser, rolling rumble.</p>
        <ul><li>Level-compensated: more Feedback makes the rumble more rolling, not louder.</li><li>The loop is damped (about 1.5 kHz), so each repeat is a little darker.</li><li>100% stays just under unity inside: even at the top, the rumble fades out by itself after the kicks stop.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">5</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Smear</h3><span class="range">size + decay, one macro</span></div>
        <p>How big and how long the cloud is. Low settings give a short, tight bloom; high settings a slow, wide swell that rings out between bars.</p>
        <ul><li>Dark FDN engines: the space grows mostly in the top half of the knob, and the decay runs from about a quarter of a second to about 5 seconds.</li><li>Long tails stack up across kicks, so the output is trimmed a little as Smear rises: Smear changes how long it rings, not how loud it is.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">3</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Drive</h3><span class="range">staged · sub joins past 3/4</span></div>
        <p>Saturation, with the type set by switch 9. Up to three-quarters of the knob, only the band above 90 Hz is driven, so the grit sits on top and the sub keeps its weight and pitch. Past three-quarters, the sub blends into the saturator too, and at full Drive the whole rumble is driven.</p>
        <ul><li>About ×6 gain at the middle of the knob, ×16 at full.</li><li>Level-matched: Drive changes character, not loudness.</li><li>Drive sits before Split. Its grit is mostly above the split, so you hear most of it on Out 2.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">6</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Split</h3><span class="range">40 Hz – 2 kHz · 24 dB/oct crossover</span></div>
        <p>Where the sound divides between the two outputs. Below the split is the rumble, on Out 1; above it is the texture, on Out 2. Noon is about 120 Hz, the usual top of a rumble.</p>
        <ul><li>The two halves add back up to the whole sound, so Out 1 and Out 2 mixed at equal level sound like one unsplit rumble.</li><li>Turn it down for a tighter, darker rumble and more texture; turn it up to put more of the sound on Out 1.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">4</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Duck hold</h3><span class="range">no gate: 5–400 ms · gate: 0.25×–4× its length</span></div>
        <p>How long the rumble stays down after each kick. With no gate patched, it's a time: set it to about the length of the kick's body, so the rumble comes back as the kick ends. Noon is about 45 ms.</p>
        <ul><li>With a gate patched, it multiplies each gate's length, so you can lengthen or shorten every duck without reprogramming the sequencer. Noon is 1×, the gate as it is, with a little play either side so it stays exactly 1× near noon. The release scales with it.</li><li>Above 1× is exact on every gate. Below 1×, the module can't know a gate's length until it ends, so it uses the previous gate's: exact on a steady pattern, approximate when step lengths vary.</li><li>The duck itself is always full: the rumble drops out completely on every hit.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">7</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Duck release</h3><span class="range">gate: 10–200% of gate · no gate: 30 ms–1.5 s</span></div>
        <p>How long the rumble takes to swell back after the hold.</p>
        <ul><li>With a gate patched, Release is a share of each gate's length, so the whole duck stretches with every step. Around 10 o'clock is 25%. The floor is 10 ms, so very short gates don't click.</li><li>With no gate, it's a time in milliseconds.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">8</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Engine switch</h3><span class="range">Sub · Texture · Mutable</span></div>
        <p>Chooses the smear engine.</p>
        <ul><li><b>Dark FDN 1 (Sub):</b> an 8-line feedback delay network built for low end. Light diffusion and slow modulation keep some punch and a steady sub pitch.</li><li><b>Dark FDN 2 (Texture):</b> the same network, larger, fully diffused, with deeper modulation and a brighter loop: a moving wash that suits Out 2.</li><li><b>Mutable:</b> the plate reverb from Mutable Instruments' Rings. Brighter and familiar.</li><li>Both Dark FDNs run on one network, so switching between them mid-sound is smooth.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">9</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Drive type switch</h3><span class="range">three types</span></div>
        <p>Chooses the saturation curve for Drive.</p>
        <ul><li><b>Tube:</b> asymmetric, adds even harmonics.</li><li><b>Tape:</b> a soft knee that rolls the top off as Drive rises: rounded, more glue than grit.</li><li><b>Wavefolder:</b> folds peaks back on themselves, for metallic growl.</li></ul>
        <p class="note"><span class="todo">Being decided</span> A pentode (hard-knee, odd harmonics) and a rectifier (octave-up grit) are being auditioned for these three positions.</p>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">10</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Tap button</h3><span class="range">a gate by hand</span></div>
        <p>Fires the duck, and holds it down for as long as you hold the button, just like a gate on the Gate input.</p>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">11</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Lights</h3><span class="range">four, left to right</span></div>
        <ul><li><b>Trigger:</b> flashes on each duck. Green when a gate fired it, pink when it was detected from the kick.</li><li><b>Gate:</b> lit while the Gate input or the Tap button is held.</li><li><b>Rumble:</b> follows the rumble's volume: bright at rest, dark on the duck, fading back up with the release.</li><li><b>Predelay:</b> pulses on each predelay repeat, each pulse dimmer by the Feedback amount. With Feedback at 0, one pulse per kick.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">12</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>CV inputs</h3><span class="range">seven, one per knob</span></div>
        <p>Each adds to its knob, so a sequencer can move hold, release or drive per step. Drive's CV sweeps through the staged range, so you can modulate from clean grit into a fully driven sub.</p>
        <p class="note"><span class="todo">To confirm</span> CV range per the Versio hardware.</p>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">13</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Gate input</h3><span class="range">rising edge fires · high holds</span></div>
        <p>Fires the duck on each rising edge and keeps it down while the gate is high. While gates are arriving, the kick detector is ignored; after 4 seconds without a gate, the duck goes back to listening to the kick.</p>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">14</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Kick Input</h3><span class="range">any level</span></div>
        <p>Your kick. The rumble path levels it automatically: it follows the loudest hit of the last 1–2 seconds and brings it to a fixed reference (up to +20 dB for quiet kicks, down to −6 dB for hot ones).</p>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">15</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Mix In</h3><span class="range">joins before the split</span></div>
        <p>A second source, mixed in after Drive and before the split. It isn't smeared or driven, so it stays clean. Noise is the classic choice: slow sample-and-hold for dark grit, white noise for air. Most noise sits above the split, so it lands on Out 2 and plays straight through; only the part below the split ducks with the rumble.</p>
        <ul><li>Normalled to the Kick Input on the panel. When it only carries the kick, it's ignored.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">16</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Out 1 · rumble</h3><span class="range">below the split · hot</span></div>
        <p>The rumble: everything below the split, ducked, with a 30 Hz high-pass to keep the deepest sub out of the way. It runs hot, so set its level at the mixer or with an attenuator.</p>
        <ul><li>A soft clipper at the end is a safety net. With default settings it barely works; with Smear, Feedback and Drive all pushed up, it adds some saturation.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">17</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Out 2 · texture</h3><span class="range">above the split · hot</span></div>
        <p>The texture: everything above the split, not ducked, with its own soft clipper. Use it as a second layer, or process it on its own: a high-pass, a delay, a different reverb. High-pass it above the kick's body (about 200–300 Hz) and it won't compete with the kick.</p>      </div></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ THE DUCK ============ -->
  <section id="duck" aria-labelledby="duck-h">
    <h2 id="duck-h">The duck</h2>
    <p>The kick and the rumble share the same low frequencies, so they take turns. On every kick the rumble drops out, then swells back in before the next one. That push and pull is what makes a rumble groove.</p>
